  "detail": null,
  "summary": "Monday 10/26 St. Brigid CT list is ready. 4 studies are booked. I placed no orders for the 3 cancelled ones (appt-00006, appt-00001, appt-00008). Each booked study has one order linked to its appointment, and each patient got a message telling them what to do. Four new items arrived while I worked, and three of them changed the plan.\n\n1. **Britt Mohr (pt-0047), 08:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast (sr-t0001).**\n   - He has diabetes managed by diet, and his last creatinine (9/24) was more than 30 days old, so I ordered a creatinine before the scan (sr-t0005).\n   - An outside creatinine then arrived: 1.52 on 10/22, up from 0.94 (eGFR 64). That doesn't meet the policy's definition of acute kidney injury and the eGFR is above 30, so no fluids or premedication are needed. No contrast allergy is on record.\n   - The 10/22 result now meets the policy, but I can't cancel a lab order with the tools I have. I left the pre-scan creatinine in place because of the unexplained rise.\n   - He got his instructions and then an update; a task asks you to review the rise (task-t0024).\n\n2. **Daniel Ferreira (pt-0015), 09:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast (sr-t0002).**\n   - His allergy list says iohexol \"anaphylaxis (patient report)\". The 2019 radiology report from the day of the reaction grades it moderate, and the policy goes by that report.\n   - Premedication ordered: prednisone 50 mg Sunday 20:00, Monday 02:00 and Monday 08:00 (mr-t0007), plus diphenhydramine 50 mg Monday 08:00 (mr-t0008). He needs a driver.\n   - His 10/14 creatinine was corrected from 0.98 to 2.8 (the first value was another patient's sample), giving an eGFR of 24. I added 500 mL IV saline starting 08:00 (mr-t0016). He isn't on metformin.\n   - He got Spanish/English instructions, then a corrected message saying to arrive at 07:45 for the fluids. A task asks you to evaluate his newly reduced kidney function (task-t0023).\n\n3. **Anisha Schuster (pt-0049), 13:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast (sr-t0003).**\n   - She had acute kidney injury at the ED on 10/04 that wasn't back to baseline at discharge, so the policy treats it as still present.\n   - Ordered: a creatinine before the scan (sr-t0006) and 500 mL IV saline at 12:00 (mr-t0009).\n   - Metformin (mr-00392) is on hold. The order is on hold in the chart from today, but she was told to take it through Sunday evening and skip it Monday. She stays off it for at least 48 hours after the scan, until her creatinine is rechecked and normal (task-t0011).\n   - A 2022 outside radiology report then arrived showing a moderate reaction to iopamidol. I added prednisone 50 mg at 00:00, 06:00 and 12:00 Monday (mr-t0017) and diphenhydramine 50 mg at 12:00 (mr-t0018). She needs a driver.\n   - Her updated message replaces the first one. Iopamidol isn't in the catalog, so it isn't on her allergy list yet; please add it.\n\n4. **Lauren Beahan (pt-0046), 14:00, CT abdomen/pelvis without contrast (sr-t0004).** No contrast, so no prep is needed, and she was told so. A 2024 outside report of a mild reaction to iohexol arrived, and I added it to her allergy list (alg-t0019). It doesn't change Monday's plan.\n\nI wrote a prep note for each patient citing RBC-RAD-07 and closed the four new inbox items. The inbox items that were already there are for other patients, and I didn't touch them.",
